# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for minimalstdlib
#
# Usage:
#   TSAN_OPTIONS="suppressions=$(pwd)/tsan.supp" ./your_tsan_binary
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hazard-pointer store-buffering (Dekker) false positive
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# include/lockfree/hazard_pointer implements hazard pointers whose
# protector/reclaimer handshake is the classic store-buffering pattern
# (Store(HP); Load(source)  vs  Store(source); Load(HP)). Its correctness
# rests on the sequential-consistency TOTAL ORDER -- every operation in the
# handshake is memory_order_seq_cst for exactly this reason.
#
# TSan's happens-before-based detector does not model the seq_cst total
# order across distinct locations, so it occasionally reports a spurious
# use-after-free between a reclaim free and a concurrent protected read
# (~1 run in 150 under heavy multi-consumer contention).
#
# This was proven to be a TSan modeling artifact, not a real bug, via a
# sanitizer-independent page-guard experiment (each reclaimed object on its
# own page, mprotect(PROT_NONE) on free, never reused): 750 clean runs of
# the real code vs ~18% faults for a deliberately-broken control. See the
# "A note on ThreadSanitizer" comment in include/lockfree/hazard_pointer.
#
# The free side of the false positive always runs through the domain's
# targeted reclaim (try_reclaim_specific); suppressing that frame silences
# the artifact. TSan symbolizes it as a bare function name (no namespace/
# class qualifier), and a use-after-free report is matched by the "race"
# suppression type.
race:try_reclaim_specific
